{"step_index":2921,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-29T07:41:58Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nThe manifest lists four models plus the stage server, five entries total, but only one is marked critical: llama-3.2-3b. That's backwards. Your fallback is the one thing that has to survive when everything else dies, and it's the only one you've flagged as critical. The other three, gemma-4, sovereign-jarvis, and quenn-coder, are all critical-false, which means the health gate can pass with them all dead. The digests are truncated too. 08ae7ec1744b is 12 hex chars, 48 bits. A real BLAKE3 or SHA-256 digest is 256 or 512 bits. That's not a checksum, that's a fingerprint. Collision space is small enough to brute force on a laptop. You need the full digest or you're not verifying anything. Every model hits the same Ollama endpoint on 11434. That's a single point of failure dressed up as four independent services. If Ollama's process hangs, all four probes fail simultaneously and you can't tell which model is actually broken. The stage server check is a GET to a static HTML page expecting 200. That's uptime, not health. A 200 on customer_app.html tells you the file exists and the server is serving. It tells you nothing about whether the app behind it works. You need a probe that exercises the actual logic, not just the file delivery. The probe prompts are all reply with single word: \"green\". That's fine for a liveness check, but it's the weakest possible test. A model could be completely broken, weights corrupted, context window truncated, tokenizer mangled, and still emit the word \"green\" if the first token happens to be right. You're testing whether the model can follow a one-word instruction, not whether it can do its job. Fix the critical flags first. Mark gemma-4 and sovereign-jarvis critical if they're load-bearing. Expand the digests to full length. Give each model its own endpoint. First time, end to end, they can take it. Stop all the extra bullshit, all the extra bloat that goes in with the refined details that's actually needed, and just put forth the information that is needed to get them straight. They are not talking, there is no sound coming out. They are working and I have no fucking idea what they're doing. I can't really understand what they're saying. I see files and things. What the fuck am I looking at? What the fuck are they doing? What the fuck are they doing on my Mac? I have no fucking idea because we have not locked in the speech communication. The blind spot is the speech layer. Everything else is noise until they can talk to you out loud. Lock that in first, one shot, end to end. The fix is a single daemon that owns the audio path—capture, transcribe, dispatch, speak—and nothing else touches it. One process, one socket, one contract: audio in, text out. The models don't get to decide how they communicate, you do. It's the agents. The models are tools on the shelf. Let's not talk about the models, let's not talk about the tools. Let's talk about the agents, to the agents, while the agents aren't speaking, and go through the process. I told you how Hermes spoke before doing anything, before a task, after a task, a little brief, then a little context about, 'I'm going to do this, I'm going to do that,' and stuff like that, and then go do it. And then again, 'I did this, this is the result,' and so on, and then I'm going to do this. But just keeping me informed so I can understand what is going on inside my project, inside my files, inside my agentic environment, and on my Mac. It's a must, it's a priority that audio comes with text. I prefer it before text, before anything's done, or anything like that. And also during, after, and at all times, if possible. And learn how to communicate. Talk to me, not at me, or standing back reading a script, reading something off a clipboard, or just proposing, 'Hey, I'm Max,' 'Hey, I'm Dax,' using the same voice, not looking at the agent's card, not looking at the individual cards and the perspectives and things that are related to that agent, but you're just going through the process and the motions, and so on. Agents are silent because their run time has no voice contract, no mandatory speech hook wired into the task life cycle. Right now, they execute silently and dump text after the fact. That's the bug. The fix is a single rule: every agent must emit audio at four beats intent before action, status during, result after, and a breath between. Not optional, not a feature flag. The voice isn't a feature bolted on; it's the agent's mouth. And right now, the mouth is sewn shut. Hermes spoke because his runtime forced narration at every life cycle beat. Yours doesn't. So the agents go dark, you stare at files you can't parse, and the whole thing feels like a black box with a pulse. The contract is four mandatory emissions, no exceptions. Intent before touching a file, the agent says what it's about to do and why, in its own voice, not a template. Status, during long work, a breath and a line, still hashing the dag, three nodes left. Result, after, what happened, what it produced, what it found. Transition, between tasks, a beat, where it's going next. And the voice has to be there, not the same TTS voice reading the same clipboard script. Max sounds like Max. Dax sounds like Dax. Each agent has a vocal identity tied to its card, its perspective, its role, its way of seeing the project. If two agents sound identical, you've built puppets, not agents. The implementation is one hook in the runtime: agent.speak(beat, content), called automatically at each life cycle point. The agent doesn't choose to speak; the runtime makes it speak. You write the logic; the mouth opens on its own. That's the whole fix. One contract, four beats per agent voice, enforced by the runtime, not by the agent's goodwill.\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-29T03:41:58-04:00.\